<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!-- Reaching the people you follow, and nothing else. No location, no
         Bluetooth, no SMS: a follower talks to a network and draws a line. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" />
    <!--
      CHANGE_WIFI_MULTICAST_STATE — so that mDNS can actually receive.
      **THE LEG OF DISCOVERY THAT ANDROID SWITCHES OFF BY DEFAULT.** p2panda
      spawns `MdnsDiscovery` in `Active` mode, and its own comment warns that
      without a mode "two peers on the same wifi never see each other". On
      Android there is a second switch: the wifi chip drops multicast to userspace
      unless an app holds a `MulticastLock`, and holding one needs this
      permission. So mDNS was running and hearing nothing.

      It cost a night to find. Two phones on one wifi, the subject's phone slept
      and came back on a new address, and the follower could no longer resolve
      its node id to anywhere — the relay leg was not connected either, so there
      was no route left at all. Both vaults went dark together, which is what
      said it was transport rather than storage.

      It is not an internet permission. It changes nothing about what leaves the
      phone; it lets this app receive the multicast the wifi chip is already
      seeing.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CHANGE_WIFI_MULTICAST_STATE" />
    <!-- Scanning an invite. Optional: an invite can be pasted as text. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CAMERA" />
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.camera" android:required="false" />

    <!-- FOUR MORE ARRIVE BY MERGE, and the shipped app asks for seven. Anyone
         reading only the three above will be surprised by the list F-Droid
         prints, so it is written down here:

           WAKE_LOCK, RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED, FOREGROUND_SERVICE
               from androidx.work:work-runtime. WorkManager holds a wakelock
               while a worker runs, and RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED is how it puts
               the fifteen-minute sync back after a reboot — without it a
               restart stops the syncing silently and the next reading you see
               looks current and is hours old, which is the failure this app
               exists to make visible.
           ${applicationId}.DYNAMIC_RECEIVER_NOT_EXPORTED_PERMISSION
               generated by androidx.core for its own non-exported receivers.

         FOREGROUND_SERVICE is the one that is NOT used: WorkManager declares
         it for expedited work, and nothing here calls setExpedited or
         setForeground. It can be dropped with tools:node="remove" — deliberately
         not done yet, because it would mean a new release while merge request
         48469 is under review and its build entries name a tag. -->

    <application
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.Follower">
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/app_name">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
    </application>
</manifest>
